# 5. Browser permission workflow

This requirement is extremely important.

I want notification permission to be requested when a user logs into the application for the first time on a browser/device.

Implement the following workflow for BOTH administrators and employees.

## First login

1. User successfully logs in using Firebase Authentication.
2. Application checks browser notification permission.
3. Application checks whether this browser/device has already been registered for the authenticated account.
4. If permission is not yet granted, display a professional in-app notification introduction.
5. Explain that notifications help users receive task assignments, status requests, deadline reminders, query responses, and other important updates.
6. Display an Enable Notifications button.
7. Only when the user clicks that button should the application invoke the browser notification permission request.
8. If permission is granted, register the service worker, obtain the FCM registration token using the supported Firebase Web SDK API, and register it with the authenticated backend.
9. Show a success state.
10. If permission is denied, explain how the user can enable it later through browser settings.

Do not repeatedly show permission prompts after the user has already made a decision.

Do not automatically request browser permission on every login.

Do not request browser permission before the user understands why it is needed.

Browser notification permission is associated with the browser origin, not an individual Firebase account. However, device tokens must be associated with the currently authenticated application user.

## Existing permission states

Handle all possible states:

- default
- granted
- denied

If permission is granted but no token is registered, attempt registration through an explicit or appropriate application-controlled registration flow.

If permission is denied, do not repeatedly trigger the browser permission dialog.

Provide an Enable Notifications or Notification Settings option where appropriate.

If the browser does not support the required Push API or Firebase Messaging APIs, display a clear compatibility message.

Never break normal application usage because notifications are unsupported or denied.

