# 7. Device token management — critical requirement

An employee may use:

- A laptop.
- A desktop.
- A personal browser.
- Another browser.
- Multiple devices.

Therefore, do NOT store only one token per employee.

Create the following Firestore structure:

users/{uid}/devices/{deviceId}

Fields:

- deviceId
- fcmToken
- platform: WEB
- browserName, if reliably available
- userAgent, if necessary and privacy-appropriate
- permissionStatus
- isActive
- createdAt
- updatedAt
- lastSeenAt
- lastSuccessfulSendAt
- lastErrorAt
- tokenVersion

Use server timestamps where possible.

Each authenticated account may have multiple active device records.

The same employee must receive notifications on all their active, registered devices.

The administrator must also be able to register multiple devices.

## Token registration endpoint

POST /api/push/register

Request must contain the device registration information.

The backend must:

1. Verify Firebase Authentication ID token.
2. Identify the actual UID from the verified token.
3. Retrieve the authoritative user profile.
4. Check account status.
5. Validate the registration token.
6. Associate the token with the authenticated UID only.
7. Create or update the device record.
8. Handle duplicate registrations safely.
9. Record timestamps.
10. Return a safe response.

Never accept an arbitrary owner UID from the frontend.

The authenticated UID determines ownership.

## Token refresh

Implement token refresh handling.

When the FCM registration token changes:

- Update the existing device record.
- Avoid leaving unnecessary stale tokens.
- Ensure future notifications use the latest valid registration token.

Do not create unlimited duplicate device records when the same device registers repeatedly.

## Logout and account switching

When a user logs out:

- Remove or deactivate the current account's association with that browser/device token through a protected backend endpoint.
- Do not delete the Firebase Authentication account.
- Do not delete the user's Firestore notification history.
- Prevent notifications belonging to the previous account from appearing after another user logs into the same browser.

When a different account logs in on the same browser, register or associate the current browser token with the newly authenticated UID only after the user has authorized notifications.

Handle this carefully because browser notification permission is origin-wide, whereas application account ownership is user-specific.

