# 8. Notification recipient architecture

This is the most important business-logic requirement.

Every notification must be delivered to the correct recipient based on the event and its associated task relationships.

Never broadcast every notification to all employees.

Never assume that everyone in a department should receive every task notification.

Never send a private employee notification to another employee.

The backend must determine recipients using authoritative Firestore records.

Use:

- Firebase UID.
- User role.
- Task ID.
- Task membership.
- Assigned employee IDs.
- Relevant administrator identity.
- Event type.

The recipient list must be calculated on the server.

## Recipient rules

### A. Task assigned to one employee

When an administrator assigns a solo task:

- Notify the assigned employee.
- Notify only their active registered devices.
- Do not notify unrelated employees.
- Do not notify all employees in the department.

### B. Task assigned to a group

When an administrator creates a group task:

- Notify every assigned employee separately.
- Retrieve each employee's registered device tokens.
- Send to each employee's active devices.
- Do not expose another employee's device token or private information.
- Maintain one shared task document with independent member records.

### C. Employee status update

When an employee changes their task status:

- Notify the responsible administrator or configured manager.
- If the task has an explicit assigned manager, notify that manager.
- Do not notify every employee assigned to the group unless the existing product behavior explicitly requires it.

### D. Employee reports a blocker

Notify the responsible administrator or manager.

Include the relevant task title and a safe description.

### E. Status request

When an administrator sends a status request:

- Notify the specific employee or selected employees.
- Each employee receives an independent notification.
- Do not combine multiple employees into one recipient record.

### F. Status response

When an employee responds to a status request:

- Notify the administrator who initiated the request or the responsible manager.

### G. Employee query

When an employee raises a query:

- Notify the responsible administrator or manager.

### H. Administrator answers a query

Notify only the employee who raised that query.

### I. Task submission

When an employee submits a task:

- Notify the administrator responsible for reviewing that submission.

For group tasks, each employee submission must generate its own appropriate event.

### J. Approval

When an administrator approves an employee's submission:

- Notify that particular employee.

Do not notify unrelated group members unless the event is explicitly intended as a group-level update.

### K. Revision request

When an administrator requests a revision:

- Notify only the employee whose submission needs revision.

### L. Deadline changed

Notify all employees currently assigned to the affected task.

### M. Employee reassignment

Notify the newly assigned employee.

Notify removed employees when appropriate.

### N. Task cancellation

Notify all currently affected assigned employees.

### O. Group communication

When a group member sends a message:

- Notify other relevant group members.
- Notify the responsible administrator if the existing notification policy requires it.
- Do not notify the sender about their own message.
- Do not send messages to unrelated employees.

### P. Reminder events

Send reminder notifications only to the employee whose individual task assignment is eligible.

Do not send one group-wide reminder to every member without checking individual submission state.

### Q. Employee account deactivation

Immediately stop sending notifications to that employee's inactive account.

Do not delete historical notifications.

