# 11. Reminder notification lifecycle

Remember the original application requirement:

Managers configure reminder times, such as 12 PM and 3 PM, using Asia/Kolkata timezone.

Each employee must be evaluated independently.

A group task has independent reminder eligibility for every member.

Do not send reminders to employees who have been removed from the task or whose accounts are deactivated.

Do not send ordinary work-pending reminders to employees whose work is already submitted.

However, remember that the original product requirement expects reminders to stop completely only after the task has been submitted AND verified by the administrator.

Therefore, distinguish these two phases:

1. Work pending: employee has not submitted.
2. Awaiting verification: employee has submitted but administrator has not yet approved.

When the employee submits, stop the ordinary work-pending reminder template.

If the configured reminder policy requires continued reminders until manager verification, use a separate, appropriately worded awaiting-verification notification.

After administrator approval, stop all recurring reminders for that employee's assignment immediately.

If revision is requested, reactivate the appropriate work-pending reminder eligibility.

Do not send reminders for cancelled tasks.

Use persistent reminder occurrence records and deduplication keys.

Do not invent a new scheduler if one already exists.

Inspect the current reminder architecture and connect FCM to its existing evaluation process.

If the existing application only supports manual reminder evaluation, preserve that behavior and clearly document that automatic scheduled delivery requires an available server-side scheduler.

Do not assume the Spark plan provides scheduled Cloud Functions.

